00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.244
into how your web
application works.

00:04:34.244 --> 00:04:35.160
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.380
So recently, we had an episode.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.120
We talked with our
colleague Aja Hammerly.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:44.660
And we discussed Stackdriver
as a monitoring tool.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.420
But now when you
introduce yourselves,

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.500
you use this other
word, diagnosis.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.450
Could you tell us a little
bit about what is diagnosis,

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.100
and how is it different
to monitoring?

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.130
SHARAT SHROFF: Got it.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:02.240
So when Aja spoke
about Stackdriver,

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.600
she covered a lot of the system
and infrastructure monitoring

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:09.670
pieces.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:13.850
And this is basically
telling the users-- typically

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:20.980
in that case, Ops, or DevOps,
or our developers-- how

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:23.900
their infrastructure is
behaving at any given time,

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.610
at one specific time.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:32.270
What Morgan and I are focused
on is basically around

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.970
when the user knows
that they have

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.230
a problem in their
application, how

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:43.520
do they go about finding the
root cause of the problem.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.020
So all the various
tools that you

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.630
need to discover exactly
where the problem is occurring

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.417
and why the problem
is occurring,

00:05:50.417 --> 00:05:52.250
that's where we are
really focused on today.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:53.010
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.551
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah,
I think it would

00:05:54.551 --> 00:05:56.490
be fair to say that one
is more Ops focused.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.610
That would be like Stackdriver
monitoring and alerting.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:00.130
It tells you when
something's wrong

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.750
and it tells you from a very
high level perspective what

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.370
is wrong and what's failing.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:07.490
And the diagnostic tools,
like Trace and Error

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.570
Reporting and Debug
can be used to actually

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.490
find out in your code or in
your architecture, the root

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.760
cause of the problem
so you can fix it.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.310
SHARAT SHROFF: And the
other way to think of it

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.020
is these are effectively two
sides of the same puzzle.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.630
So when you have a problem
with your software,

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.620
you look at it in
two different places.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.260
Is there a problem
in my system, or is

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.970
the problem in my application?

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.260
And based on that, you
need different tools.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.070
You need different tools
to find out why and where

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.240
the problem is occurring.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.160
And so much of what
we're talking about

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.310
is at the application
level, which

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.452
tends to be-- which
is basically around,

00:06:48.452 --> 00:06:50.410
how do you connect the
code to what is actually

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.772
happening in production?

00:06:52.772 --> 00:06:53.980
MARK MANDEL: All right, cool.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.090
So I guess at a high level,
what tools are available?

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:01.590
Like say something goes wrong.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.190
I'm getting errors in my code.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:07.240
Where does that lead me
to within Stackdriver?

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:11.900
SHARAT SHROFF: So
today, we've got

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:16.380
a few tools that are out there
that users can actually use.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.070
Logging of codes--
what I consider

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.420
logging is-- logging
is the one place,

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.886
is the one tool that every
application developer can use.

00:07:25.886 --> 00:07:27.760
If they don't have any
other tools available,

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.360
you can always go to logging,
because you can always go back

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:34.400
to your code, instrument
your code to output something

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.550
to logs, then go back
and look at logs.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:40.109
You can probably build some
kind of an automation to scrape

00:07:40.109 --> 00:07:41.650
through logs, and
so on and so forth.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:44.170
Or even better, the
one really cool feature

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:47.790
that exists today in
Stackdriver is to take the logs,

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.160
export it to BigQuery.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.990
And you then have the
full power of BigQuery

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.290
to analyze your logs.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:54.960
So that is always there.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.160
In addition to logs,
we have some tools

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.770
like Trace-- and Morgan can
tell you a lot about Trace.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:07.210
We have Error Reporting,
which basically,

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:12.160
if you think of Trace as
the view of the application

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.950
from a latency point of view,
Error Reporting is really

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:19.830
looking at your
application errors

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.610
no matter where
they're occurring,

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.080
and be able to analyze your
application almost instantly.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.652
And then the fourth tool
that you have available

00:08:28.652 --> 00:08:32.350
is the debugger,
Stackdriver Debugger,

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:37.360
which effectively takes a
snapshot of your application

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.220
at runtime.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.190
It doesn't pause the
application or anything.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.669
It almost at runtime takes a
snapshot of your application

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:45.600
and allows you to inspect
the state of the application

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.039
as the application is
serving out requests.

00:08:48.039 --> 00:08:49.830
So those are the tools
that you have today.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.020
There is more coming
online, but this is the four

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.487
I can talk about right now.

00:08:54.487 --> 00:08:55.445
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:08:55.445 --> 00:08:56.111
All right, cool.

00:08:56.111 --> 00:08:59.320
So let's take a
couple of scenarios

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.840
and talk through them, and
see what tools we end up.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:02.500
So, OK.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.060
The classic example, I
guess, is I'm getting

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:06.300
an error in my application.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.300
Where does that go?

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.190
Does it go to logging?

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:13.035
Does it automatically get
caught by Error Reporting?

00:09:13.035 --> 00:09:14.410
I've written some
code in my app,

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.750
but I don't know-- where
does that information go to,

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:17.666
and how can I find it?

00:09:17.666 --> 00:09:19.660
How can I know something
bad has happened?

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.240
SHARAT SHROFF:
Yeah, so errors can

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.580
manifest in a couple of
different ways, right?

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:28.390
The one sure place where it
will manifest is in logs.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.290
But error reporting is
always scraping through logs.

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:36.820
So the moment you
have an application,

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:38.290
an App Engine--
there is no setup

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:43.000
needed at all, by the way,
for any of these tools.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.480
There is no additional step of
integrating agents or anything

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:46.980
like that.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.040
If you have an App
Engine application, boom.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:50.770
Error Reporting
will work for you.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.640
And the moment you
have your application

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.880
and it starts throwing
out some kind of errors,

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:02.320
Stackdriver Error Reporting
will be able to immediately,

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:05.980
one, identify that it's
a new type of error,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:09.510
and alert you that an
error has occurred,

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.560
and three, give
you more statistics

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:12.860
about that error itself.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:14.700
How often that
error is occurring.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.430
It can give you a
distribution of that error.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:20.141
Is it happening 10 times a
second, or 10 times a week,

00:10:20.141 --> 00:10:20.890
or whatever it is.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.110
You have all the
statistics available just

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:24.770
by going to one URL.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.000
So you would get notified.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.740
So in this scenario
that you described,

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.450
you would see a
notification that

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.250
tells you that, hey, there's
a new error that has occurred.

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:39.060
And then you would basically
go in and click on that link,

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.510
and you would then get all
the details about that error,

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:46.180
including capturing
the call stack.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.980
So you will basically--
associated with that error,

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:50.810
we capture the
entire call stack.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.510
Not only do we capture
the call stack,

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:57.910
we actually pause through
that call stack and identify

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.170
the areas which are
most likely of interest.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:02.960
Because we look at
the call stack--

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:04.960
whenever I look at a call
stack, my eyes kind of

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.135
glaze over because it's huge.

00:11:07.135 --> 00:11:09.300
It's insane, right?

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.777
So what Error Reporting
does really well

00:11:11.777 --> 00:11:13.110
is it basically says, OK, great.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:14.900
We have sifted through
the call stack.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:17.910
Here are these five lines that
you need to really look at.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.790
And you have everything
else available.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:24.070
If you want to, but it basically
hints to the user, saying,

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.430
thse are the five lines
that you've got to look at.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:27.270
MORGAN MCLEAN: And
what I've found--

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:28.720
part of the beauty
of Stackdriver

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:30.220
and the Stackdriver
suite of tools

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.430
is in situations like
this, where maybe there's

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.120
an error occurring
in your application,

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.840
so you find it in
Error Reporting.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.147
Or you've been notified that
there's very high latency

00:11:38.147 --> 00:11:38.980
in your application.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.352
You can actually jump
between the tools, right?

00:11:41.352 --> 00:11:43.060
So in the experience
for Error Reporting,

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.070
I believe you can
launch into Debug.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:48.330
In Trace, for example, if you
find a very high latency trace,

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.660
you can launch into the
logs for those traces.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.140
And so it's not so much
a collection of products

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:55.890
that you're using, or
a collection of four

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.810
different features that
Stackdriver offers to you,

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:01.470
it's really a suite
for diagnostics.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:01.970
Right?

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.230
So even once you
isolate the problem

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.100
with one of the
lenses in that suite,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.290
whether that be logging
or error reporting,

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.480
you can then follow up
and get more analysis

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.015
from some of the other
functionality that we offer.

00:12:13.015 --> 00:12:14.890
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah, I
think Morgan brings up

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:17.250
a really interesting point,
here, and a really valuable

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.207
point.

00:12:18.207 --> 00:12:19.790
If we were going
about building tools,

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:21.660
we would just keep
churning out tools.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.020
But the real thing is
we're really focused

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.160
on developer, DevOps workflows.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.000
It's really understanding,
OK, something bad

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.770
is happening with
your application,

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:34.260
and you have been notified.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.144
Or should we eventually
notify you, and where

00:12:36.144 --> 00:12:37.060
you're being notified.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:41.370
We aspire to hold your
hand right from the point

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.660
where you first get
that notification

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:47.430
and take you all the way to
where the problem is occurring,

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:50.200
while, at the same time,
corralling information

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:53.530
from the application, from
the user themselves as they're

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:56.960
navigating through the journey,
and from the system when

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:01.230
we have it, which is why
we're all under Stackdriver.

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:05.560
And where possible,
the application's

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.830
customers, the
application's users.

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:10.640
So in App Engine, for
example, Error Reporting

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:15.310
can tell you how many
customers have been impacted

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:16.870
by this specific error.

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:20.680
So we try to combine all
this information in order

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:22.270
to help you do two things.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:26.236
Find you, bring you to the root
cause, and help you prioritize.

00:13:26.236 --> 00:13:28.860
Do I need to drop everything and
immediately jump in right now,

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.630
or is it something that I
can wait and address later?

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.130
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah,
and some cases,

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:34.964
there are even suggestions
on how to fix it.

00:13:34.964 --> 00:13:36.379
MARK MANDEL: Cool,
so that means I

00:13:36.379 --> 00:13:38.870
guess I could get an error
notification through Error

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.787
Notifications that could then
make me go look at some logs.

00:13:41.787 --> 00:13:43.370
Maybe from there as
well, I could jump

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.410
into either Trace or Debug.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:48.110
I can jump around
as necessary to see

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:49.419
where that thing's happening.

00:13:49.419 --> 00:13:50.210
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yep.

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:53.714
And there's further integration
coming in as we keep building.

00:13:53.714 --> 00:13:54.630
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.780
There's a step that I'm
missing out, though, which is,

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.700
so you say that you can
jump directly from the Error

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.540
Reporting to the code.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:05.440
How is the code linked
to your project?

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.540
Could you talk a
little bit about that?

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:09.690
How do I do that?

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:13.790
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah, so the
place, the linkage point

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:17.280
occurs typically at the call
stack level, where we basically

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.710
show you a line number, a
filename or the line number.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:27.810
And if the user, or
if the project has

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:32.070
the source code
linked to the app--

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:34.400
then we can actually do this.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.570
And there are three
different sources right now,

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.880
three different repos
that we support.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:41.930
Of course, there's the
Google Cloud repository.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:45.930
So if your code is hosted
in Google's Cloud repo,

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:50.500
then we are able to,
from the call stack,

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:55.490
infer the correct version
of the application code,

00:14:55.490 --> 00:15:00.110
and basically allow you to
navigate directly from the call

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:02.960
stack to the application
code, and, with one click,

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.330
allow you to take a snapshot.

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:07.890
And I'll describe a little
bit later about what

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:09.630
the snapshot really does.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.740
But in addition to
that, if your code

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:16.280
resides in GitHub or
Bitbucket, then you can link.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:20.310
It's a very straightforward way
to link your code that resides

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.590
in GitHub repo or Bitbucket.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:28.230
But the moment you do that,
then Stackdriver Debugger

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:34.610
will very easily be able
to navigate to the source.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.750
That's one of the--
the magic sauce

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:41.025
that exists in all of this.

00:15:41.025 --> 00:15:43.275
MARK MANDEL: So that's that
sounds really interesting.

00:15:43.275 --> 00:15:47.210
Are there any limitations that
people should be aware of,

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:50.980
maybe by either language or it
only works in certain products?

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:52.770
SHARAT SHROFF: Right
now, much of what

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:57.310
we're talking about works
very well for almost all

00:15:57.310 --> 00:16:00.430
the major languages
in App Engine.

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:04.310
We are very rapidly
building out the same kind

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:09.980
of support for Compute
and Container Engine.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.280
And in Compute
Engine, you will see

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:15.000
the matrix, the
language support matrix,

