from Logging, Trace, Debugger,
Error reporting, all of these

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:13.210
have APIs that's all available
through the one platform API.

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:17.600
So if you want to be able
to extract traces or write

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:19.400
traces-- we'll talk
about write as well.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:23.320
But if you want to
extract any of this data

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:27.780
so that you can host it in your
own dashboard, you can do that.

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:30.745
And so that's something
to think about as well.

00:20:30.745 --> 00:20:33.370
MORGAN MCLEAN: Or if you want to
submit data to those services.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:36.600
But perhaps we haven't
provided a library yet.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:39.160
You're certainly welcome to
do so through those APIs.

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:42.120
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah, I mean,
if you think of the example

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:46.410
where-- not everything is
traditional request responses,

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:46.910
right?

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:52.030
So you could have a scenario
where a developer is trying

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:56.810
to diagnose latency of
one specific function

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:59.550
that he or she has written down.

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:04.040
So you have the ability to
say, here is a custom span that

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:06.170
and I would like to capture.

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:09.100
And you insert a start
time and an end time.

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:10.960
That is not something
that is automatically

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:14.610
instrumented for you, but
a developer can do it.

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:17.035
So she or he can
basically say, here

00:21:17.035 --> 00:21:19.210
is a custom start
and a custom stop,

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:22.440
and have this whole
thing span represented

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:25.420
in the context of my entire
request so that they can say,

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:27.990
this function adds
x milliseconds

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:28.925
to my overall request.

00:21:28.925 --> 00:21:30.800
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah,
and that would be useful

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:31.750
