00:21:42.185 --> 00:21:44.310
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So what
you were talking about now

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:46.385
made me think about a
best use case, which

00:21:46.385 --> 00:21:50.620
is I have one new
version of my code.

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:54.000
And I want to make sure
if it's faster or slower.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:56.060
How could I go to do that?

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:59.040
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah, so
I think you'd probably

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:01.470
want to start in Trace
because this is primarily

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:03.090
a latency analysis.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:05.627
And Trace actually has
some pretty unique features

00:22:05.627 --> 00:22:06.710
that allow you to do this.

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:09.570
So in the past, or if you're
using other tools, what

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:11.490
you might do is take
a collection of traces

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:13.630
before and after the
