00:22:39.755 --> 00:22:41.710
So let's say you rolled
out a new version

00:22:41.710 --> 00:22:44.280
and you didn't do or take
any additional actions.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:46.960
Next time you go to
the Cloud Console,

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:49.620
you'll see that there's
actually a comparison

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:53.550
in that section of
the Trace UI that

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:54.870
did a comparison day by day.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:56.370
And if the latency
has significantly

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:59.120
changed either for
better or for worse-- but

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:01.980
if the profile of
latency has changed,

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:03.580
we will both alert
you and give you

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:07.680
sample traces that let you do
that comparison for yourself.

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.150
Secondly, if you want to
do a manual report, which

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:11.870
you can do immediately--
so you don't

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:15.230
have to wait for the next
automated one to come through.

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:18.670
You can trigger a manual
report to do either a version

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:20.820
by version comparison,
or a time comparison.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:23.740
Let's say you could
compare yesterday's traces

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:27.320
to all of the traces collected
over the last month on average.

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:29.550
And it will run that same
analysis report for you

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.300
and tell you if there's
been a significant change

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:33.258
in the profile of latency
for your application,

00:23:33.258 --> 00:23:35.890
or for a particular component
of your application.

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:37.640
Or you can do it per
endpoint or something

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:40.850
like that, if you have
microservices architecture.

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:43.780
And it'll also, once again,
give you those sample traces

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:45.970
so you can actually
get examples, and maybe

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:47.930
click through on a
few and go, oh, maybe

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:50.226
the database or something
slowed down after we

00:23:50.226 --> 00:23:51.350
rolled the version forward.

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:53.410
Or calls to this
service slowed down.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:57.030
And so you can actually very
rapidly isolate what changed.

00:23:57.030 --> 00:24:00.300
And then you can use that
to jump into other tools

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:04.880
like Debug to find out
specifically why it changed.

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:06.740
SHARAT SHROFF: And
Error Reporting as well

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:08.210
is version-aware.

00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:12.790
So the moment you push out
a new version and a new kind

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:15.104
of error, or a new
error starts occurring,

00:24:15.104 --> 00:24:16.270
it's automatically captured.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:18.860
And you always have the ability
right there on the top--

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.110
MORGAN MCLEAN: You can filter.

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:21.530
SHARAT SHROFF: You can
filter down by version.

00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:23.860
So you can say, here is
the version A, version B.

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:27.790
But it's good to also
highlight at this point

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:32.060
the underlying
emphasis on trying

00:24:32.060 --> 00:24:36.910
to remove some of the
operational friction

00:24:36.910 --> 00:24:39.850
where we would
prefer, we would like

00:24:39.850 --> 00:24:44.510
to remove some of this what
I would call grunt work so

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:48.200
that developers can focus
all their effort on creating

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:51.541
while Google's got their back.

00:24:51.541 --> 00:24:52.040
Right?

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:57.120
Basically, when a new
version rolls out,

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:58.050
we'll be aware of it.

00:24:58.050 --> 00:24:59.550
And not only will
we be aware of it,

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.366
we'll always compare it
with what's happening,

00:25:02.366 --> 00:25:03.490
with your previous version.

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:06.667
And if we see significant
amount of divergence

00:25:06.667 --> 00:25:08.250
in any of these,
whether it's latency,

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:10.270
or availability,
or errors, we'll

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:11.805
immediately service that app.

00:25:11.805 --> 00:25:15.960
We'll always be able
to link those errors

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:19.010
to the right
version of your code

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:22.750
so that we can allow you to
very quickly navigate through

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:23.962
to the problem.

00:25:23.962 --> 00:25:24.920
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:29.560
Well, we're running out
of time, but what could

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:33.830
you recommend for our listeners
if they have never tried any

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:36.320
of the Stackdriver features?

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:37.965
What is the first
thing they should do?

00:25:37.965 --> 00:25:39.840
MORGAN MCLEAN: Well, if
you're on App Engine,

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:42.690
you should just go use it.

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:45.540
We have pretty good
adoption on App Engine,

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:48.532
but there's no reason to not
use Stackdriver on App Engine,

00:25:48.532 --> 00:25:50.240
because you get all
these tools for free.

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:54.430
We're already collecting
instrumentation data.

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:57.940
If you have a large,
important set of applications

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:00.580
on App Engine, you should go
try out the Stackdriver suite

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:01.860
immediately.

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:03.860
And I think you'll find
that it's really useful.

00:26:03.860 --> 00:26:04.440
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:06.690
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah, the
other thing I would emphasize

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:17.780
is, it's as simple as clicking
through to a tab on the Cloud

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:19.990
Console, and you'll
be able to see.

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:23.546
It's all under the
Operations tab group.

00:26:23.546 --> 00:26:25.170
And you'll just be
able to click on it,

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:27.070
and everything
should be working.

00:26:27.070 --> 00:26:29.610
And once you do
that, everything else

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:33.550
is just turned on automatically
for App Engine standard.

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:36.860
And we will continue to have
that same level of focus

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:42.450
towards minimal amount of setup
needed so that users can use

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:44.980
that when we have the same
offerings available in Compute

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.865
and Containers, and [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:46.865 --> 00:26:48.490
MARK MANDEL: So yes,
we are running out

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:49.573
of time just a little bit.

00:26:49.573 --> 00:26:51.140
But is there anything
else you'd like

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:53.419
to add that we haven't
either covered, or maybe

00:26:53.419 --> 00:26:54.960
haven't mentioned,
or something you'd

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:58.390
like to plug about Stackdriver
or Stackdriver diagnostics

00:26:58.390 --> 00:26:59.520
stuff.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:03.930
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah, so the one
thing that I want to emphasize

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:06.820
is, all the features
that you hear

00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:09.260
about in diagnostics,
whether it's logging,

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:13.040
or Trace, Debug,
Error Reporting.

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:15.840
All of these features
that we enable completely,

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:17.370
automatically out
of the box, we are

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:23.330
obsessive about performance of
the overall application itself.

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:28.810
What I mean by that is the
footprint that we use in order

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:31.870
to do this-- the
amount of overhead that

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:35.280
is added to these applications
in order to capture all

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:39.490
these diagnostics is in
the order of microseconds,

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:42.830
or single-digit millisecond
is the way to think of it.

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:45.640
So we want to make
sure that we are not

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.780
adding an additional burden
to the application itself.

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:52.940
The second thing is, basically
every one of these things

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:53.950
that we capture.

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.430
Every one of these pieces of
information that we capture

00:27:56.430 --> 00:28:00.420
is basically
curated, is basically

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:06.820
analyzed by some of
our [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:09.210
One example to think
of is Trace insights.

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:12.070
We have about a billion traces,
more than a billion traces,

00:28:12.070 --> 00:28:13.920
easily more than a
billion traces coming

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.760
into our system every day.

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:21.782
Each one of these
traces is analyzed

00:28:21.782 --> 00:28:24.360
to identify points
of suboptimality

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:25.860
within that, so
that we can actually

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.284
say, hey, I'm making too many
datastore [INAUDIBLE] calls

00:28:28.284 --> 00:28:30.450
MORGAN MCLEAN: Or your
memcache calls are very slow.

00:28:30.450 --> 00:28:31.590
SHARAT SHROFF: Or memcache
calls, or something like that.

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:33.400
So we identify
every single trace

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:36.930
so that we can then provide,
service all of that information

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:38.950
to the developer.

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:47.830
The third thing to call out
is, if you want to basically go

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:50.740
from identifying a
problem, or identifying

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:53.850
the location of a problem,
to the root cause,

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:58.010
there isn't a better
tool than Debugger.

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:01.960
The way to think of the
debugger is it basically--

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:07.310
I think one of the engineers
who wrote this tool describes it

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:07.810
really well.

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:10.230
It's like taking an
x-ray of a person who

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.200
is running without
stopping the person, right?

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.900
You can see the internals
of the entire system.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:20.070
I've got some of the best
stories around very interesting

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:25.520
bugs that Debugger has
uncovered just by the ability

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:29.320
to have a very detailed
view into the working

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:32.620
of the application without
ever pausing this application.

00:29:32.620 --> 00:29:34.309
MORGAN MCLEAN: It's
like a snapshot.

00:29:34.309 --> 00:29:35.600
SHARAT SHROFF: It's a snapshot.

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:38.660
And to reproduce a
similar functionality,

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:41.060
it often takes developers days.

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:44.405
One way to think of it is
very often, if you're in Debug

00:29:44.405 --> 00:29:46.680
and try to find the
root cause of a problem,

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:49.500
you would go instrument
some lines of code.

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:52.260
You would again go through
the validation and deploy.

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.260
Again, go back and
analyze the code.

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:56.050
Again, do this
thing multiple times

00:29:56.050 --> 00:29:58.510
until you've probably
found the right thing.

00:29:58.510 --> 00:30:00.930
With Debugger, [INAUDIBLE]
while the problem is occurring.

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:02.971
Even if it's happening in
production, no problem.

00:30:02.971 --> 00:30:04.190
Just click, take a snapshot.

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:04.940
Boom.

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:06.910
You'll very quickly
be able to say, ah,

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:09.510
there is a risk condition
that I didn't see.

00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:12.340
You don't have to reproduce that
anywhere in a test environment.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:13.100
MORGAN MCLEAN: You
don't have to redeploy.

00:30:13.100 --> 00:30:15.730
SHARAT SHROFF: You just
debug in production, right?

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:17.830
And that just is spectacular.

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:22.910
When customers use it
and I hear the wow,

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:25.689
it's very exciting for
us building these tools.

00:30:25.689 --> 00:30:27.480
MORGAN MCLEAN: And
correct me if I'm wrong.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:30.900
I think that actually works
across App Engine and GCE

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:31.812
and GKE, right?

00:30:31.812 --> 00:30:35.250
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah, many
GCE languages are already

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:38.650
supported, so many of this
is available in GCE and GKE

00:30:38.650 --> 00:30:40.105
as well.

00:30:40.105 --> 00:30:40.980
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:43.580
Well yeah, like I said, I think
we are running out of time.

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:46.020
So I think we're going
to have to cut it there.

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:48.640
But thank you very much to
both of you for joining us.

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:49.640
We really appreciate it.

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:50.540
MORGAN MCLEAN:
You're very welcome.

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:51.540
Thank you for having us.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:52.990
SHARAT SHROFF: Thank you, folks.

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:54.906
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Thanks
again to Sharat Shroff

00:30:54.906 --> 00:30:58.690
and Morgan McLean for sharing
their very deep knowledge

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:03.190
on Stackdriver, especially on
the diagnostics parts of it.

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.292
I discovered many
different tools

00:31:05.292 --> 00:31:07.500
that I'm going to be using
next time I have an issue.

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:09.965
I'm not looking forward
to having any issues,

00:31:09.965 --> 00:31:11.820
but for next time,
I know what to do.

00:31:11.820 --> 00:31:13.695
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, no
I actually kind of want

00:31:13.695 --> 00:31:15.930
to sit down with Debugger
now and just poke at my app

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:18.550
and just see what it's doing,
just at a given point in time.

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:20.633
FRANSESC CAMPOY: I had
some fun with the debugger.

00:31:20.633 --> 00:31:22.720
And I got to say, it's
pretty impressive.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:25.200
Basically, instead of just
adding more and more logs,

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:29.220
which is normally what I
do-- that's how I do it.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:31.830
Rather than just logging every
single value in your function,

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:33.610
you just put a
little breakpoint.

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:37.600
And you don't actually
stop it, so it is awesome.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:38.710
MARK MANDEL: Perfect.

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:40.120
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So let's go
with the question of the week.

00:31:40.120 --> 00:31:41.730
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, you
posed a really interesting

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:42.940
on when we were
sitting here earlier.

00:31:42.940 --> 00:31:45.023
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
the question of the week

00:31:45.023 --> 00:31:49.284
is, OK, so I've been reading
about Twelve-Factor-- not

00:31:49.284 --> 00:31:50.310
authentication again.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:53.360
Twelve-Factor applications.

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:56.130
And by the way, if you
don't know what they are,

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:58.199
you should just
search that on Google

00:31:58.199 --> 00:31:59.490
and read a little bit about it.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:00.370
MARK MANDEL: We'll put
it in the show notes.

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:02.036
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
one of the things

00:32:02.036 --> 00:32:05.440
that the Twelve-Factor
application manifesto says

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.260
is that you should always deploy
basically the same artifact

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:08.760
everywhere.

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.990
So the same compiled binary,
or the same docker image,

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:13.960
or whatever you're doing.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:17.700
And the differences between
QA and test and production

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:20.670
should be done with
environment variables.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:23.370
So the question is,
how do you do that?

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:25.890
How do you do
environment variables

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:27.010
on Google Cloud Platform?

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:28.530
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and
there's a few different ways

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.740
you can do it depending on
which product you're on.

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:32.115
FRANSESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, basically.

00:32:32.115 --> 00:32:37.080
So depending, if you have GCE,
GAE, and GKE-- so if you have--

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:38.830
MARK MANDEL: Say that
three times quickly.

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:40.120
FRANSESC CAMPOY: No,
I cannot do that.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:41.670
I'm pretty surprised
I did it once.

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:46.050
So on App Engine,
there's actually a thing

00:32:46.050 --> 00:32:48.170
called environment variables.

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:50.360
So you can set them
very easily inside

00:32:50.360 --> 00:32:53.450
of the app.yaml or
app.xml, depending

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:54.930
on the language you're using.

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.640
And basically, it
allows you to say, hey,

00:32:57.640 --> 00:33:00.950
you know the variable
name-- I don't know.

00:33:00.950 --> 00:33:01.600
Not host.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:03.170
That's a bad idea.

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:06.770
But let's go with something like
database URL equals something.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:10.190
And then when you access it
from your App Engine code,

00:33:10.190 --> 00:33:13.110
it actually looks like it's
an environment variable.

00:33:13.110 --> 00:33:15.870
So every language has different
ways of accessing those.

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:19.130
But basically, it will behave
like any other environment

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:20.100
variable.

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:23.245
So what about on GKE,
on Container Engine?

00:33:23.245 --> 00:33:25.460
MARK MANDEL: So GKE,
you have a few options.

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:27.420
Quite similarly to App
Engine, when you're

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:29.620
sending your YAML files
or your configuration

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:34.650
up to your Docker containers,
or GKE itself up to Kubernetes,

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:36.600
you can explicitly set
environment variables

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:38.970
in that YAML file.

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:41.940
But if you want something
that's maybe a little bit less

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:44.210
coupled, a little
bit more flexible,

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:47.210
you can do things like
config maps or secrets.

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:50.380
Those are ways to send up
information up to Kubernetes

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:52.560
with a given name that can
then be looked up later.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:54.310
Those can be exposed
in a variety of ways,

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:56.272
from volumes and a
variety of other things.

00:33:56.272 --> 00:33:57.730
But one of the ways
you can do that

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:01.030
is sending up data as
environment variables.

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:04.270
Secrets tend to be used when
you've got private information,

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:05.890
like usernames and passwords.

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:08.590
Config maps are used
when you have tech stuff,

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:10.520
like maybe, where is
my database pointed at?

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:13.069
Or maybe, where is the
URL for my mailing list?

00:34:13.069 --> 00:34:15.610
Or maybe it's just like, hey,
this is the version of this app

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:16.568
that I want to display.

00:34:16.568 --> 00:34:18.739
It's got a blue button
rather than a red one.

00:34:18.739 --> 00:34:20.630
Less sensitive stuff.

00:34:20.630 --> 00:34:23.370
So you have a variety
of options there.

00:34:23.370 --> 00:34:26.650
Now on Compute Engine, so if I'm
running just a straight-up VM,

00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:28.770
is there an option
that I can use there?

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:31.270
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So one of the
things that you can imagine

00:34:31.270 --> 00:34:34.260
is you log into your
instance and you just

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:36.360
set the environment
variable there.

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:39.949
Whatever OS you're running-- so
let's say you're using Linux.

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:42.479
You go export, whatever,
equals whatever.

00:34:42.479 --> 00:34:43.270
And that will work.

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:47.110
But next time you
start a new instance,

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:48.616
it will not have those values.

00:34:48.616 --> 00:34:50.949
So how do you do it if you
want to set those environment

00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:54.000
variables across
all your instances?

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:58.830
Well, the best way is actually
using the metadata server,

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:00.710
metadata server API.

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:03.365
And is just a key value store.

00:35:03.365 --> 00:35:04.870
So it is very simple.

00:35:04.870 --> 00:35:08.140
And whenever you are
going to set a new one,

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:10.170
all of the instances
that are already running

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.570
will have access
automatically to that value.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:14.640
So it can be very
useful for if you're

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:17.180
doing something
like the database,

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:18.670
like a link to the database.

00:35:18.670 --> 00:35:21.669
And if at any point you
need to change that,

00:35:21.669 --> 00:35:22.460
you just change it.

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:26.570
And if your code is regularly
checking that variable out,

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:29.220
everything will just work.

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:31.860
It is also completely safe,
so you can store secrets

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:32.870
if you want to.

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:35.490
It is encrypted, so there's
no problem with that.

00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:38.830
And finally, the cool thing
is that this will actually

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:40.640
work anywhere.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:43.920
So you can access
those variables

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:47.780
from Compute Engine,
obviously, but also

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:50.572
from App Engine and
Container Engine.

00:35:50.572 --> 00:35:52.780
MARK MANDEL: Now do I need
to call an API to do that,

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:54.577
or is that just
inherently available?

00:35:54.577 --> 00:35:56.410
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So if
you're in App Engine,

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:58.550
you need to use an API.

00:35:58.550 --> 00:35:59.860
It is very simple.

00:35:59.860 --> 00:36:02.005
It's an [INAUDIBLE] API.

00:36:02.005 --> 00:36:06.620
And I'm almost sure
there's a library for that.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:10.160
But even if there was
not, it's just basically a

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:13.080
get request onto
a URL that is only

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:15.390
accessible from inside
Google Cloud Platform.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:16.990
So basically, only
you have access.

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:17.220
Yeah.

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:18.800
MARK MANDEL: I can do exactly
the same thing from a Docker

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:20.350
container inside GKE as well.

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:21.480
Perfect.

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:23.310
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it is very simple,

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:25.310
but it is incredibly powerful.

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:28.590
And you can use it from
anywhere in your projects.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:31.890
So it makes a very easy way
to integrate different pieces

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:33.200
of your architecture.

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:33.690
